# [[.Name]]

A clean Go engineering skeleton generated by nostosx-cli. This application owns
its source. Ordinary builds and runtime do not depend on the CLI or nostosx-starter;
contract development calls the CLI without copying its tool source. Example
business applications remain in the separate Starter project.

[简体中文](README.zh-CN.md)

## Start

Use Go **1.27.3**, Make and `GOWORK=off`. Download pinned public dependencies:

```sh
export GOWORK=off
go mod download
export API_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
```

`API_TOKEN` must contain at least 32 printable ASCII characters without whitespace.
Keep it private. Commands below use POSIX shell; on Windows use Git Bash.

[[if eq .Layout "monolith"]]This layout includes a runnable empty host:

```sh
go run ./cmd/server
```

In another terminal:

```sh
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/readyz
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/livez
```

There are no business routes. Stop with Ctrl+C. The default HTTP and gRPC ports
are 8080 and 9090; override with `-addr` and `-grpc-addr`.
[[else]]This layout starts without service processes. Add a context and service using
the next section, then run the generated entry point. Shared transport and optional
agent packages can already be built and tested.
[[end]]
HTTP exposes health checks without authentication. gRPC health requires
`authorization: Bearer <API_TOKEN>` metadata. Both listeners are plaintext and
restricted to literal loopback IPs. See [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) before exposing
application methods or deploying across hosts. There is no default database,
migration, business API, project metadata file or archtest package.

## Add a context and service

Set `NOSTOSX` to the absolute path of the CLI binary built from nostosx-cli.
From this project's root:

```sh
export NOSTOSX=/absolute/path/to/nostosx
"$NOSTOSX" add context order --dir .
"$NOSTOSX" add service order --dir .
```

`add context` creates package declarations in:

```text
domain/order/doc.go
application/order/doc.go
application/order/command/doc.go
application/order/query/doc.go
```

No use cases, repositories or fake data are generated. Keep domain code independent
of transport and persistence. Define real typed commands, queries, handlers and
stable operation names with `github.com/nostosx/servex/cqrs` in the application
layer when needed. Select logging, metrics and tracing middleware at service
assembly; `github.com/nostosx/servex-contrib/middleware@v1.1.0` provides typed CQRS
middleware if required. It is not an initial dependency.

`add service` requires the same-named context. It creates:

```text
api/proto/order/v1/order.proto        empty OrderService contract
api/http/routes/order.yaml           guidance for HTTP bindings
api/http/operations/order.yaml       guidance for response options
services/order/module.go             explicit registration function
services/order/module_test.go        registration regression test
services/order/internal/port/rpc/     protobuf adapter
services/order/cmd/order/main.go      independent process entry point
```

Install generation tools once:

```sh
go install github.com/bufbuild/buf/cmd/buf@v1.72.0
```

Put Buf on PATH. Contract builds run through the CLI, without application-local
tool source or Python. Then generate, tidy and run:

```sh
make generate
go mod tidy
go run ./services/order/cmd/order -addr 127.0.0.1:8081 -grpc-addr 127.0.0.1:9091
```

The empty service only registers its contract and process health. Context/service
additions create new files and never rewrite existing wiring or dependencies.

To add an API to the service:

```sh
"$NOSTOSX" add api create-order --service order --method POST --path /v1/orders --dir .
"$NOSTOSX" add api get-order --service order --method GET --path '/v1/orders/{order_id}' --dir .
make generate
go get github.com/grpc-ecosystem/grpc-gateway/v2@v2.29.0
go mod tidy
make api-check
```

`add api` inserts the import and RPC into `api/proto/order/v1/order.proto`. It
creates `create_order.proto` beside it, the HTTP binding at
`api/http/routes/order_create_order.yaml`, and `create_order.go` plus its test
under `services/order/internal/port/rpc`. The API name is a lowercase hyphenated
slug; the target must be an existing generated v1 service. Methods are GET, POST,
PUT, PATCH or DELETE. Paths accept literal segments or simple `{field_name}`
parameters, which become string request fields. Other request/response fields
remain application-owned. POST/PUT/PATCH use `body: "*"`.

The `go get` step pins the gateway runtime to the generator version; the initial
skeleton does not need it. Review compatibility before changing an existing
application that already uses a newer runtime.

The RPC returns `Unimplemented`; implement it and replace the stub test with real
behavior tests. Map Proto messages to independent application CQRS use cases as
needed. No HTTP handlers are mounted by this command: configure authentication
and authorization and register the generated gateway handlers in `module.go`
explicitly. OpenAPI output alone does not expose a live endpoint. Response options
remain in `api/http/operations/`.

The command refuses existing outputs, conflicting symbols/Handler methods and
overlapping routes across services. Inline `google.api.http`, wildcard selectors,
custom methods and complex path patterns require manual maintenance. Only the
target service Proto is replaced, after validation and new-file creation; normal
failures roll back owned outputs. Interrupted processes can leave partial files
and a lock; inspect those before retrying.

For a gRPC-only API, omit both `--method` and `--path` from `add api`.
It creates messages and the adapter/test, without an HTTP binding or gateway
runtime requirement. The HTTP flags must be provided together.

If you write the Proto contract first, use the CLI to fill missing unary methods:

```sh
"$NOSTOSX" api scaffold --service order --dir .
make generate NOSTOSX="$NOSTOSX"
go mod tidy
"$NOSTOSX" doctor --dir . --json
```

Scaffolding runs offline with local imports under `api/proto` and built-in
Protobuf well-known types. Vendor other imports locally. It preserves matching
implementations and only creates missing method files returning `Unimplemented`;
repeating it adds nothing. It does not add stub tests, HTTP bindings or business
use cases. Implement authorization and application calls, then add behavior tests.
Streaming methods, incompatible signatures, build constraints, platform files and
output conflicts require manual handling. Signatures must use concrete generated
message types, without type aliases or dot imports. Scaffolding shares the project
lock and normal-error rollback with other additions.

Doctor reads project configuration and trusted Go/Buf version commands, without
installing tools, fetching modules or modifying files. It checks Go 1.27.3 or
newer and Buf 1.72.0 when Proto files exist; the empty skeleton skips Buf.
Use text output by omitting `--json`. Failed checks exit with code 1.
Doctor does not replace `make check` or `make api-check`.

[[if eq .Layout "monolith"]]To run `order` inside the monolith, import `[[.Module]]/services/order` in
`cmd/server/main.go` and replace the `nil` registration argument to
`bootstrap.RunCLI` with `order.Register`. For several services, pass a function
matching `bootstrap.Register` and call each registration in sequence, returning
errors. The standalone entry point remains available; choose one deployment
arrangement rather than starting duplicate instances unintentionally.
[[else]]Add more same-named contexts/services as needed. Run each entry point in a
separate terminal with distinct `-addr` and `-grpc-addr` values. Services share
one Go module but have independent process lifecycles. Keep service-private
adapters under `services/<name>/internal`; call another service through an explicit
client port, not its private implementation.
[[end]]
## Contracts

The Makefile calls CLI v1.0.0 through `go run`, outside this application's
dependency graph. Until that release is published, or when testing a source
build, point `NOSTOSX` at the absolute binary path:

```sh
export NOSTOSX=/absolute/path/to/nostosx
make generate api-check NOSTOSX="$NOSTOSX"
```

`NOSTOSX_VERSION` selects another reviewed release. Ordinary builds, tests and
runtime do not invoke the CLI. Contract operations are also available directly:

```sh
nostosx api generate --dir .
nostosx api lint --dir .
nostosx api check --dir .
nostosx api breaking --dir . --against '.git#ref=main'
```

The initial project has no Proto source and needs no Buf for its first build.
`make api-check` accepts that empty state. Once services exist:

```sh
make api-lint
make generate
go mod tidy
make api-check
```

Generation pins Buf 1.72.0, protoc-gen-go v1.36.12, protoc-gen-go-grpc v1.6.0 and
grpc-gateway/openapiv2 v2.29.0. Plugins run through `go run` with explicit versions
and may download modules. Go/gRPC/gateway output belongs in `api/gen/go/`;
`api/http/openapi.swagger.json` uses OpenAPI 2 (Swagger 2.0). Do not edit generated
files. Empty services have no gateway methods or business paths in OpenAPI.

Contracts and HTTP configuration are transport boundaries. Application and domain
packages must not import generated protobuf or gRPC types. Map request/response
messages in `services/<name>/internal/port/rpc`. Use action-and-object filenames
for real use cases and matching tests, with small consumer-owned interfaces.

Contract generation and CLI additions share `.nostosx.lock`. Wait for its owner;
remove a stale lock only after confirming that process has stopped. Generation
preserves previous output if plugins fail and removes stale output after success.
`api check` compares generated paths and bytes without replacing published files.
Normal publication errors attempt rollback; process termination can leave a lock
or staging directory. Trust the project configuration and Go/Buf tools: contract
commands execute plugins and are not a sandbox for untrusted source.
For compatibility checks, first establish a versioned Git contract baseline, then
run `make api-breaking API_BASE=<revision>`; no baseline is created for you.
